 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets for change delivery.** Committed changes are pushed to connected browsers over WebSockets we operate. Five-second HTTPS polling was rejected for its repeated reads and multi-second lag; a hosted realtime service was rejected for its recurring fees and provider coupling. Self-hosted sockets deliver fast updates without a realtime vendor, accepting responsibility for connection capacity and reconnect behavior. This obligates replayable revisions and reliable publication in the change store, session management in delivery, and reconnect/catch-up logic in browser sync. See [Change delivery approach](decisions/change-delivery-approach.md).
